# Jain Philosophy: Many-Sided Truth and Non-Violence

> Jainism teaches that reality has endless facets, that karma is
> literal matter binding the soul, and that non-violence is the
> supreme duty — held more strictly than in any other tradition.

## 1. The Tirthankaras

A **Tirthankara** ("ford-maker") is one who crosses the river of
becoming and shows others the crossing. The tradition counts **24
Tirthankaras**:

| Tirthankara | Position | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rishabhanatha (Adinatha) | First | Legendary first teacher of the era |
| Parshvanatha | 23rd | Historical; taught four restraints |
| Mahavira | 24th | 540–468 BCE (traditional dates); contemporary of the Buddha |

**Mahavira** was born at Kundagrama near Vaishali; he renounced the
world at thirty, attained omniscience (**kevala-jnana**) after twelve
years of austerities, and reorganised the community. Where Parshvanatha
had taught four vows, Mahavira added **brahmacharya** (celibacy) as the
fifth.

## 2. Anekantavada: The Many-Sidedness of Truth

**Anekantavada** holds that reality has infinitely many aspects and no
single statement captures it whole. Every judgement is made from some
partial standpoint (**naya**). Its logical expression is **syadvada**,
the sevenfold predication, where every claim is qualified by *syat* —
"in some respect":

1. In some respect, it is.
2. In some respect, it is not.
3. In some respect, it is and is not.
4. In some respect, it is indescribable.
5–7. The three further combinations of these, taken together.

[example]
Blind men touch an elephant — the side, the tusk, the trunk — and each
declares "the elephant is like a wall," "like a spear," "like a snake."
Each is right from his standpoint; each is wrong about the whole.
[/example]

## 3. What There Is: Six Substances

Reality divides into **six substances (dravyas)**:

| Dravya | Kind | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Jiva (soul) | Living | Conscious; countless, each eternal |
| Pudgala (matter) | Non-living | Has touch, taste, smell, colour |
| Dharma | Non-living | Medium that enables motion |
| Adharma | Non-living | Medium that enables rest |
| Akasha | Non-living | Space that accommodates all |
| Kala | Non-living | Time, the support of change |

Unlike Buddhism, Jainism insists the **jiva** is a genuine agent: the
soul *acts*, *enjoys*, and *suffers* — consciousness is its essence,
not a by-product of matter as Charvaka held.

## 4. Karma as Matter and the Seven Tattvas

Jain karma is **literal, subtle matter (pudgala)** that flows into the
soul and binds it, obscuring knowledge and energy. The path is mapped
in **seven tattvas**:

@diagram tree id="philosophy-jain-path" layout="tree" aria="The seven Jain tattvas from influx of karma to liberation"
  "Seven tattvas" -> Jiva
  "Seven tattvas" -> Ajiva
  "Seven tattvas" -> Asrava
  "Seven tattvas" -> Bandha
  "Seven tattvas" -> Samvara
  "Seven tattvas" -> Nirjara
  "Seven tattvas" -> Moksha
@/diagram

| Tattva | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Jiva | The living soul |
| Ajiva | The non-living substances |
| Asrava | The inflow of karmic matter into the soul |
| Bandha | Bondage — karma sticking to the soul |
| Samvara | Blocking new inflow |
| Nirjara | Shedding accumulated karma through austerities |
| Moksha | Liberation when all karma is gone |

[important]
When every particle of karma is shed, the soul — now pure, omniscient,
and all-powerful — rises to the summit of the universe, Siddhashila.
This state is **kaivalya**: perfect isolation and release.
[/important]

## 5. Ethics: The Five Great Vows

| Vow | Meaning | Jain strictness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ahimsa | Non-violence | Monks sweep the path, strain water, cover the mouth |
| Satya | Truthfulness | Speech that harms is not truth |
| Asteya | Non-stealing | Not even what is freely dropped |
| Brahmacharya | Celibacy | Added by Mahavira |
| Aparigraha | Non-possession | Renouncing property and attachment |

Compare the Buddhist five precepts: non-violence, truthfulness,
non-stealing, sexual misconduct (not total celibacy for laypeople), and
abstinence from intoxicants. Jain ethics presses each vow further —
non-violence covers all living beings from insects to plants.

[warning]
Santhara or sallekhana — the religious fast unto death — is accepted
only under strict conditions (terminal illness or old age), as the
final shedding of the body, not suicide in the Jain view.
[/warning]

## 6. The Three Jewels and Liberation

Liberation rests on the **ratnatraya** — right faith (samyak
darshana), right knowledge (samyak jnana), and right conduct (samyak
charitra). Note the contrast: the Buddhist **Triratna** is the Buddha,
the Dhamma, and the Sangha — a refuge in persons and community, while
the Jain three jewels are qualities of the seeker.

## 7. Canon and Legacy

The Jain canon (**Agamas**), composed in Prakrit and Ardha-Magadhi,
was compiled — tradition says — at the council of Pataliputra around
300 BCE under Sthulabhadra. The community later split:

| Sect | Name means | Practice |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Digambara | "Sky-clad" | Monks go nude, owning nothing |
| Svetambara | "White-clad" | Monks wear white robes |

[summary]
Jainism counts 24 Tirthankaras ending with Mahavira (540–468 BCE).
Anekantavada and syadvada teach that truth is many-sided. Six
substances constitute reality; the soul is real and conscious, bound
by karma understood as matter. The five vows, led by extreme ahimsa,
and the three jewels lead to kaivalya — the soul's complete release.
[/summary]
